WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.680
Star Trek: Picard has come and gone and the&nbsp;
biggest surprise of the season might be that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.209
crew of the Enterprise-D got a happy ending. But&nbsp;
could there be more adventures in their future?

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:13.500
If every Star Trek fan had sat down and watched&nbsp;
the Star Trek: Picard Season 3 finale at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.980
same time, their collective sighs of relief&nbsp;
at the end of the episode would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.360
deafening. Despite numerous close calls and&nbsp;
fake-outs, the returning cast of Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.740
The Next Generation all made it out of the&nbsp;
season-long caper alive. The uplifting ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.940
enjoyed by these characters has certainly been&nbsp;
welcomed by fans, but some may be wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.360
how none of the original Next Generation&nbsp;
crew bit the dust throughout the story.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:37.200
In an interview with Inverse, Picard&nbsp;
showrunner Terry Matalas spoke about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.100
the creative team's decision to keep all&nbsp;
the classic crew members alive. He said:

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:43.980
"Honestly, it would be very easy to&nbsp;
kill off one of these characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.360
and it would be entirely appropriate. But I&nbsp;
didn't have it in me to do it. In a weird way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.240
I wanted the surprise ending to&nbsp;
be that it was a happy ending."

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.120
A desire for a happy ending might&nbsp;
have been the main reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.940
the writers ended up keeping every&nbsp;
Next Generation crew member alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.500
but there may also be another reason for the&nbsp;
decision. The Picard finale is meant to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.058
a final farewell to several major characters,&nbsp;
including the titular captain himself. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.058 --> 00:01:10.080
there's a slight chance that Matalas might return&nbsp;
to a few of the characters in a future project.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.540
In the finale's shocking post-credits scene, Jack&nbsp;
Crusher encounters Q who he believed to be dead.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.280
"Young mortal, you have much ahead of you."

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.120
Q cryptically suggests that Jack is&nbsp;
due for a trial, leaving a dangling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.320
plot thread that would be ripe for a Picard&nbsp;
spin-off to tackle. Matalas tells Inverse:

00:01:28.559 --> 00:01:31.319
"I know how this would all go if&nbsp;
we were lucky enough to come back."

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:35.880
If Matalas ends up developing another series, it's&nbsp;
quite possible that the plot he envisions could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.200
involve characters from The Next Generation. The&nbsp;
same is true for the broader sphere of Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.100
projects. By keeping the classic crew alive,&nbsp;
there's always the potential that they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.180
be brought back for another adventure if the&nbsp;
situation calls for it. In essence, Picard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.020
gave its characters a happy ending with just&nbsp;
enough narrative wiggle room to bring them back.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.220
It seems that many veteran cast members of Star&nbsp;
Trek: The Next Generation aren't ready to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.360
goodbye to their characters just yet either.&nbsp;
During the release of Picard's third season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.560
Patrick Stewart was candid about returning to&nbsp;
the franchise once more. He told reporters:

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.920
"There is still enormous potential&nbsp;
for matters in what we can do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.260
there are doors left open and&nbsp;
we didn't close all of them."

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.160
Several months later, Stewart expressed interest&nbsp;
to Variety in returning for a Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.920
Legacy spin-off along with Ed Speelers. He noted:

00:02:17.086 --> 00:02:20.146
"If I can occasionally crop up to&nbsp;
offer a little bit of comedy myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.146 --> 00:02:21.600
then I shall be happy to do that."

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.860
Several of Stewart's fellow Next Generation&nbsp;
actors have voiced similar wishes. Jonathan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.360
Frakes seems keen on returning as Riker, as&nbsp;
he tweeted a vague message indicating that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.585
Star Trek: Picard might not be his final&nbsp;
go-around with the franchise. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.585 --> 00:02:38.760
LeVar Burton and Brent Spiner said in a joint&nbsp;
interview with Variety that, while they have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.680
reservations about returning beyond Picard, they&nbsp;
aren't shutting the door completely. Spiner said:

00:02:43.805 --> 00:02:47.705
"LeVar and I've talked about the fact that&nbsp;
if this lands in a way that we hope it lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.705 --> 00:02:51.420
maybe it would be best to be the finale. And&nbsp;
if there should be something more than this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.980
well, as he said, that would just be another gift&nbsp;
that we can't really contemplate at the moment."

